This network revolves around two incoming materials, solder_paste and steel_rod, feeding production at the Integration Center, with finished goods then moving on to the Logistics Center. The product mix is fairly broad: a frame_structure is built from 5 units of steel_rod and 3 units of solder_paste, each solar_panel then consumes 1 unit of frame_structure, each laptop uses 4 units of solder_paste, and each tablet uses 3 units of steel_rod. On the sourcing side, solder_paste is brought in on a periodic schedule, with replenishment lots typically around 69541.0 units and varying by about 16355.0 units, and those deliveries arrive every 29.0 days. Holding solder_paste costs $5.112 per unit, shortages run $5.549 per unit, and stock is reviewed every 8 days. Steel_rod follows a min-max style rule: when inventory drops below 4624.25 units, it is replenished up to 6536.76 units. Its carrying cost is $3.243 per unit, shortage cost is $20.697 per unit, and review happens every 28 days. Supplier coverage is diversified. GlobalComponents supplies solder_paste with an exponential lead time averaging 2.0 days, charges $25.461 per unit, offers 32.0-day payment terms, and can supply up to 349274 units per order. MegaSupply also ships solder_paste, with lead time between 10.0 days and 27.0 days, at $167.221 per unit, with payment due 19.0 days after receipt. GlassCore Ltd. provides solder_paste as well, with lead time averaging 25.0 days, a unit cost of $19.922, 31.0-day payment terms, and capacity of 269542 units. FastChip Corp. is another solder_paste source, delivering in 7.0 to 10.0 days, charging $84.396 per unit, and using payment terms somewhere between 10.0 days and 54.0 days. For steel_rod, SiliconWorks delivers with an average lead time of 12.0 days, costs $124.641 per unit, allows payment in 11.0 to 78.0 days, and is limited to 231404 units per delivery. Memory Star supplies the same material in exactly 15.0 days at $169.281 per unit, with payment due between 50.0 days and 54.0 days, and capacity of 299377 units. NanoMaterials Inc. rounds out the steel_rod base with a fixed 23.0-day lead time, a price of $95.622 per unit, payment in 24.0 to 61.0 days, and a maximum order size of 762046 units. Inside the plant, the Integration Center manages solar_panel and frame_structure inventory through a fabrication step that converts frame_structure into solar_panel in 9.0 to 17.0 days and requires the CNC Machine. That CNC Machine has capacity for 3 units, works with a service time ranging from 1.14 days to 8.934 days, and costs $4.565 per day to operate. The same Integration Center also runs a molding process that turns solder_paste into laptop in a fixed 25.0 days, and a cutting process that converts steel_rod into tablet in 14.0 to 24.0 days. Starting stock is substantial: 1888137 units of frame_structure, 235729 units of solar_panel, 978221 units of laptop, and 878910 units of tablet. Carrying frame_structure costs $8.202 per unit, shortages cost $12.031 per unit, and it is reviewed every 1 day. Laptop inventory has a shortage penalty of $40.392 per unit and is also checked every 1 day, while tablet inventory carries a holding cost of $5.84 per unit and is reviewed every 1 day. Outbound demand is customer-specific. TechCorp buys solar_panel, with orders arriving on an exponential pattern averaging 27.0 days; order size is roughly 3879827.0 units with variability of 80594.0 units. Customers wait 18.0 days for delivery, any shortfall is backordered, the selling price is $804.012 per unit, and payment comes in 19.0 to 28.0 days. Infineon Technologies purchases laptop every 31.0 days, with demand around 1246307.0 units and variation of 64195.0 units. Delivery lead time ranges from 3.0 days to 17.0 days, unmet demand is lost, the sales price is $1895.735 per unit, and payment is received after 34.0 days. NXP Semiconductors takes tablet orders with interarrival averaging 7.0 days, and demand ranges from 1995149.0 units to 3901855.0 units. Delivery takes 17.0 days, available stock is shipped while the remainder is lost, the item sells at $1856.924 per unit, and cash is collected after 58.0 days. Structurally, all 7 suppliers—GlobalComponents, SiliconWorks, MegaSupply, GlassCore Ltd., FastChip Corp., Memory Star, and NanoMaterials Inc.—feed the Integration Center, and the finished solar_panel, laptop, and tablet flow from the Integration Center to the Logistics Center. The model runs for 200 days, with results taken after a 30-day warm-up period, and uses 1 replication.

