Transform the following supply chain scenario into a structured JSON configuration. A schema template follows the description:


This network makes CPUs using three incoming materials—resin, plastic_pellet, and aluminum_sheet—with PolymerSource, ElectroParts, and MetalFab Inc. all feeding the Production Center, and finished CPU units then moving on to the Distribution Center. Inside the plant, two in-house built sub-assemblies support the final product: motor_assembly consumes 1 unit of aluminum_sheet, 5 units of resin, and 4 units of plastic_pellet, while heat_sink uses 2 units of plastic_pellet, 4 units of aluminum_sheet, and 4 units of resin. The finished CPU itself combines 1 unit of heat_sink, 4 units of resin, 3 units of plastic_pellet, 4 units of aluminum_sheet, and 2 units of motor_assembly. Resin is managed with a min-max style replenishment rule, triggering supply when stock drops below 595.97 units and bringing it back up to 781.71 units. That material comes from PolymerSource in anywhere from 4.0 days to 17.0 days, costs $132.182 per unit, is paid 30.0 days after delivery, and the supplier can provide up to 814933 units per order. Resin inventory is reviewed every 21 days, with a holding cost of $0.653 per unit and a shortage penalty of $27.375 per unit. Plastic_pellet is supplied on a regular schedule, with batch quantities centered around 11521.0 units and varying by about 1938.0 units, and those deliveries arrive every 7.0 days flat. ElectroParts ships that item in exactly 10.0 days, charges $171.701 per unit, and offers 49.0-day payment terms. Its inventory is checked every 26 days, carrying costs run at $6.155 per unit, and shortages cost $18.741 per unit. Aluminum_sheet is sourced on demand from MetalFab Inc., with a reliable 4.0-day lead time, a purchase cost of $403.109 per unit, payment due somewhere between 28.0 days and 83.0 days after receipt, and a maximum order size of 755370 units. Aluminum_sheet stock is reviewed every 14 days and held at $6.96 per unit. On the shop floor, the Production Center handles aluminum_sheet, heat_sink, CPU, resin, motor_assembly, and plastic_pellet inventory and runs a fabrication step that takes 4.0 days flat per cycle, using the Laser Cutter. That Laser Cutter has capacity for 1 unit at a time, works with an exponential service time averaging 5.798 days, operates at $13.589 per day, stays up for 323.117 days at a stretch, and when it fails it is down for 41.38 days. A Quality Scanner is also in the system with capacity of 1 unit, an exponential service time with a mean of 8.483 days, operating cost of $18.929 per day, uptime averaging 353.148 days, and downtime of exactly 29.133 days. Starting inventories are very large: 942430 units of motor_assembly, 2713214 units of heat_sink, and 3317896 units of CPU. Motor_assembly is reviewed every 1 day, with holding cost of $2.794 per unit and shortage cost of $19.047 per unit. Heat_sink is also reviewed every 1 day and held at $2.283 per unit. Finished CPU inventory is reviewed every 1 day as well, with holding cost of $1.689 per unit and shortage cost of $30.251 per unit. On the demand side, Bosch Automotive places CPU orders with exponentially distributed arrivals averaging 41.0 days, each for 706514.0 units, and expects delivery in between 6.0 days and 28.0 days. Bosch pays between 10.0 days and 28.0 days after receipt, and the selling price is $138.828 per unit. If stock is short, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. NXP Semiconductors, by contrast, orders every 17.0 days exactly for 1462871.0 units, accepts lead times between 3.0 days and 11.0 days, pays in 81.0 days, and buys at $4195.341 per unit. For NXP, whatever is available ships immediately and the balance is backordered. The model runs for 365 days, and results are measured after a 50-day warm-up period.

