Return a structured configuration for the supply chain described below. The expected JSON schema is provided after the description:


This network centers on making smartphones, with raw inputs coming in as microprocessor, carbon_fiber, and glass_fiber, and a fabricated sub-component called cooling_unit feeding the final build. Each cooling_unit consumes 4 units of carbon_fiber, 3 units of glass_fiber, and 5 units of microprocessor, while each smartphone requires 2 units of microprocessor, 3 units of cooling_unit, 4 units of carbon_fiber, and 2 units of glass_fiber. At Advanced Fab, the welding step combines microprocessor, cooling_unit, carbon_fiber, and glass_fiber into smartphone output, and every production cycle takes exactly 29.0 days using the Quality Scanner. That scanner has a capacity of 5 units, a fixed service time of 7.572 days, runs for 389.599 days flat before failing, and then is down for an exponentially distributed average of 41.631 days; it also costs $5.66 per day to operate. Advanced Fab manages inventory for microprocessor, cooling_unit, glass_fiber, carbon_fiber, and smartphone, and finished smartphones are then moved from Advanced Fab to the Storage Facility. On the sourcing side, PolymerSource supplies microprocessor directly to Advanced Fab with a consistent 4.0-day lead time, at $273.864 per unit, with payment due between 17.0 days and 34.0 days after receipt, and it can supply up to 325402 units per order. Microprocessor supply is triggered by demand, with a holding cost of $9.905 per unit, a shortage cost of $24.362 per unit, and inventory reviewed every 26 days. FastChip Corp. sends carbon_fiber to Advanced Fab with lead times averaging 9.0 days, charging $396.126 per unit and offering payment terms anywhere from 23.0 days to 73.0 days; carbon_fiber itself is replenished on a regular schedule every 14.0 days, in quantities ranging from 44776.0 units to 77669.0 units, with a holding cost of $4.841 per unit, a shortage cost of $41.961 per unit, and a 3-day review cycle. Glass_fiber comes from CarbonSupply Co. in between 7.0 days and 8.0 days, at $335.502 per unit, with payment due somewhere between 7.0 days and 66.0 days, and the supplier is limited to 126724 units per delivery. Its stock follows a min-max style rule: when inventory drops below 907.56 units, it is replenished up to 2081.64 units; holding cost is $0.208 per unit and reviews happen every 21 days. On-hand stock starts very high for the in-process cooling_unit inventory at 3708797 units, with a shortage penalty of $5.59 per unit and daily review every 1 day, while finished smartphone inventory begins at 4155514 units, with holding cost of $3.602 per unit, shortage cost of $6.607 per unit, and the same 1-day review rhythm. Demand comes from two customers. Lenovo Group orders smartphone with arrivals averaging every 32.0 days, and each order is between 1422271.0 units and 2981141.0 units; delivery to that customer takes exactly 9.0 days, unmet demand is backordered, the selling price is $4634.83 per unit, and cash is collected between 39.0 days and 83.0 days later. HP Enterprise also buys smartphone, with orders arriving on average every 30.0 days and fixed demand of 4276498.0 units per order; shipment lead time ranges from 5.0 days to 16.0 days, available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered, the price is $1147.914 per unit, and payment comes 66.0 days after delivery. The modeled network explicitly includes the three suppliers—PolymerSource, FastChip Corp., and CarbonSupply Co.—plus the two facilities, Advanced Fab and Storage Facility. The study runs for 730 days, with results taken after a 30-day warm-up period.

