 copper_wire. There, an inspection step converts lithium_cell, frame_structure, and copper_wire into medical_device in a fixed 27.0-day cycle. The Production Center manages lithium_cell, solar_panel, memory_chip, and copper_wire, and its welding step turns memory_chip, copper_wire, and lithium_cell into solar_panel in exactly 10.0 days. A shared Assembly Line with capacity for 5 units supports operations with a service time varying from 1.136 days to 3.398 days and an operating cost of $15.87 per day. Inventory starts quite high for downstream items: 1863546 units of frame_structure, 1366917 units of medical_device, and 4164603 units of solar_panel. Frame_structure is reviewed every 1 day and carries a holding cost of $1.788 per unit. Medical_device is also reviewed every 1 day, with holding cost of $1.505 per unit and shortage cost of $10.077 per unit. Solar_panel follows the same 1-day review rhythm, with holding cost of $4.553 per unit and shortage cost of $18.704 per unit. In terms of flow, NanoMaterials Inc., MetalFab Inc., and FastChip Corp. feed the network’s facilities — Processing Unit, Production Center, and Storage Facility. Medical_device moves from the Processing Unit to the Storage Facility, and solar_panel moves from the Production Center to the Storage Facility. On the customer side, Sony Electronics places orders for medical_device every 30.0 days, with demand centered around 4568297.0 units and varying by about 513997.0 units. Delivery to Sony Electronics usually takes between 14.0 days and 25.0 days. The selling price is $2805.039 per unit, and payment is received somewhere between 16.0 days and 75.0 days after shipment. If inventory is short, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. Apple Supply buys solar_panel every 18.0 days in a fixed quantity of 4748301.0 units, expects delivery in 6.0 days to 12.0 days, pays $553.815 per unit, and settles invoices in exactly 49.0 days. Any unmet solar_panel demand is backordered rather than lost. The model runs over a 365-day horizon.

