This network is built around producing tablets, with NanoMaterials Inc., PrecisionParts, Memory Star, ElectroParts, and GlobalComponents feeding the Pilot Line, and finished tablets then moving on to the Distribution Center. The raw inputs are copper_wire, microprocessor, and steel_rod. Upstream, circuit_board is made using 2 units of steel_rod, sensor_array uses 4 units of microprocessor, and frame_structure uses 3 units of copper_wire. The finished tablet itself is a fairly component-heavy build: 1 unit of sensor_array, 4 units of microprocessor, 3 units of steel_rod, 4 units of copper_wire, 3 units of circuit_board, and 3 units of frame_structure go into each unit.

The sourcing setup is mixed. Copper_wire is bought on an as-needed basis, with a holding cost of $1.467 per unit per week, a shortage cost of $44.916 per unit, and stock reviewed every 25 weeks. That material comes from two suppliers: NanoMaterials Inc., where lead time is exponentially distributed with an average of 28.0 weeks, the purchase cost is $87.612 per unit, payment is due between 12.0 and 60.0 days after delivery, and supply is capped at 681549 units per order; and ElectroParts, which offers a fixed 5.0-week lead time, a lower cost of $22.764 per unit, 58.0-day payment terms, and capacity of 904227 units per delivery. Microprocessor supply works differently, arriving on a regular schedule with order quantities centered around 10538.0 units and varying by about 3020.0 units, with replenishment arrivals somewhere between 9.0 and 15.0 weeks. Its carrying cost is $7.547 per unit per week, and it is reviewed every 21 weeks. PrecisionParts supplies microprocessor with exactly 19.0 weeks of lead time, a price of $119.593 per unit, net 11.0 days payment, and a maximum order size of 179428 units; shipments from PrecisionParts to the Pilot Line take between 7.0 and 13.0 weeks to transfer. GlobalComponents is the second microprocessor source, with lead time ranging from 8.0 to 19.0 weeks, a cost of $112.444 per unit, 36.0-day payment terms, and capacity of 282415 units. Steel_rod follows a min-max style replenishment logic, with a reorder trigger at 3103.36 units and an order-up-to level of 9119.81 units. Its holding cost is just $0.088 per unit per week, and reviews happen every 8 weeks. Memory Star supplies that item in a consistent 13.0 weeks, charges $22.125 per unit, allows 24.0 days to pay, can provide up to 502557 units per order, and the transfer to the Pilot Line takes 11.0 weeks.

Inside the operation, the Pilot Line is the manufacturing site managing microprocessor, circuit_board, tablet, frame_structure, sensor_array, steel_rod, and copper_wire. The fabrication step consumes sensor_array, microprocessor, steel_rod, copper_wire, circuit_board, and frame_structure to produce tablet, and each run takes between 4.0 and 26.0 weeks. That work depends on the CNC Machine, which has capacity for 2 units at a time and a service time fixed at 9.379 weeks. The machine is not perfectly reliable: it runs for an average of 134.375 weeks before failing and then stays down for an average of 47.7 weeks. Operating it costs $16.928 per week. Starting stocks are very large for the semi-finished items, with 1649412 units of circuit_board, 3543405 units of sensor_array, and 4473301 units of frame_structure on hand. Their inventory policies are reviewed every 1 week. Circuit_board carries a holding cost of $7.231 per unit per week and a shortage cost of $3.682 per unit. Sensor_array has a holding cost of $4.35 per unit per week. Frame_structure instead highlights shortage exposure, at $20.517 per unit, with its stock also checked every 1 week. Finished tablets begin with 600745 units in inventory and incur $1.478 per unit per week in holding cost, again with weekly review every 1 week.

On the demand side, all three customers buy tablet. Lenovo Group places orders between every 9.0 and 20.0 weeks, with demand roughly 1415192.0 units on average and variability of 358440.0 units. Customers are promised delivery in 8.0 to 11.0 weeks, and if inventory is short, available units ship immediately while the rest is backordered. Lenovo pays 22.0 days after receipt, and the selling price is $3935.036 per unit. HP Enterprise orders less frequently, between 21.0 and 36.0 weeks, but in bigger volumes averaging 4660223.0 units with variation of 1306903.0 units. Delivery to HP takes between 8.0 and 29.0 weeks, and the policy is partial fulfillment with the remainder lost rather than backlogged. The sales price there is $395.133 per unit, with payment due 7.0 days later. Dell Technologies follows a fixed 39.0-week ordering pattern, with demand ranging from 1343141.0 to 2895146.0 units. Delivery lead time runs from 14.0 to 29.0 weeks, shortages are also handled by shipping what is available and losing the rest, and payment comes between 26.0 and 47.0 days after delivery at a price of $3819.615 per unit.

In terms of physical flow, copper_wire from NanoMaterials Inc. and ElectroParts, microprocessor from PrecisionParts and GlobalComponents, and steel_rod from Memory Star all move into the Pilot Line, while completed tablets are sent from the Pilot Line to the Distribution Center. The model runs in weeks over a 200-week horizon with 10 replications.

